# To make into sausage.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=M[ilburg] F[rancisco] Mansfield|author2=B[lanche] McM[anus]|authorlink2=Blanche McManus|title=Romantic Ireland|volume=II|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Louis Coues Page|L[ouis] C[oues] Page]] & Company|year=1904|page=99|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/romanticireland02mansuoft/page/99/mode/1up|passage=There is no escaping the Limerick pig. In single file, in battalions, as solitary scout, alive or dead, baconed and '''sausaged''', he dominates the town.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Dion Fortune|authorlink=Dion Fortune|title=The Sea Priestess|location=York Beach, Me.|publisher=[[w:Red Wheel/Weiser|Samuel Weiser, Inc.]]|year=1938|year_published=1989|page=245|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/seapriestess0000fort/page/245/mode/1up|isbn=0-87728-424-5|passage=I mayn’t know much about pigs, but I know a lot about Muckley, and there must be something pretty wrong with any pigs that he wouldn’t risk '''sausaging'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Landmark|publisher=Waukesha County Historical Society|year=1965|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nBErAQAAMAAJ&q=%22sausaging%22|passage=At butchering time, they kept three pigs for their own use, smoking, brining, and '''sausaging''' the meat, and trying the lard.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Susan Quinn|authorlink=Susan Quinn|title=A Mind of Her Own: The Life of Karen Horney|publisher=Summit Books, [[w:Simon & Schuster|Simon & Schuster, Inc.]]|year=1987|pages=246–247|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mindofherownlife00quinrich/page/247/mode/1up|isbn=0-671-47518-5|passage=Long afterward Renate remembered “the pigs squeaking and jerking while coming down a funnel in which they were shorn…on their way to getting quartered and '''sausaged'''” and the elevator that “released one steer at a time” to be “greeted with a blow of a sledge hammer on his head.”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Leslie Land|title=High on the Hog|journal=[[w:House & Garden (magazine)|House & Garden]]|volume=164|issue=9|month=September|year=1992|page=62|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/housegarden164julnewy/page/n343/mode/1up|column=2|passage=So I took the lot and had much of it custom-smoked, then I roasted, fried, grilled, sautéed, stir-fried, stewed, braised, and '''sausaged''' the rest.}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} To form a sausage-like shape, with a non-[[uniform]] [[cross section]].

